<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Apollonia Pontica</h1><p class="st-meta">610 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.) · capital Apollonia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Milesian colony on the Black Sea coast of Thrace whose colossal statue of Apollo was carried off to Rome in 72 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> founded Apollonia c. 610 BCE on a peninsula and island on the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, at modern Sozopol in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, it became one of the leading Greek cities of the Thracian coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Apollonia traded wine, oil and fine pottery with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom</a>, for grain, metals and honey. Its coins carried an anchor. In the 5th century BCE it set up a bronze statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calamis">Calamis</a>, about 13 metres high. It was governed as an oligarchy and later a democracy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 72 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-terentius-varro-lucullus">Marcus Lucullus</a> captured Apollonia during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a> and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> statue to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The city then came under Roman control and survives as Sozopol.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/apollonia-pontica.jpg" alt="Apollonia Pontica" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Nenko Lazarov · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sozopol2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>610 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Apollonia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Apollo</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sozopol</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-341&amp;focus=apollonia-pontica" aria-label="Apollonia Pontica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5351 669 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Apollonia Pontica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5671 925L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5671 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5671 925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-341&amp;focus=apollonia-pontica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

